Also, this comment:

```js
// Keep generating food while it overlaps the snake
```

means that the loop repeats **while an overlap exists**. It does not mean the apple is allowed to overlap. Once `some()` returns `false`, the loop stops and returns a free position.

Your final function is:

```js
const generateFood = () => {
    let newFood;

    do {
        newFood = {
            x: Math.floor(Math.random() * tileCount),
            y: Math.floor(Math.random() * tileCount)
        };
    } while (
        snake.some((segment) => {
            return segment.x === newFood.x &&
                   segment.y === newFood.y;
        })
    );

    return newFood;
};
```

So the implementation is working as intended. The next useful improvement would be preventing the apple from appearing on the snake **after the snake moves into its position**, but that situation is normally handled by the snake eating the apple when its head reaches it.

This happens because the keyboard listener can process several arrow presses between two game-loop updates. The snake may receive multiple turns before it has moved, creating an unexpected direction.

Use a **direction queue with one turn allowed per movement interval**.

