Use CSS positioning. The game container should be the **positioning reference**, and the start screen should be placed above it.

In [style.css](</Users/nolannlescop/Documents/International/Courses/Web programming I/AI assignment/style.css>), add:

```css
.game {
    position: relative;
    text-align: center;
}

#startScreen {
    position: absolute;
    z-index: 10;
    top: 50%;
    left: 50%;
    transform: translate(-50%, -50%);

    width: 280px;
    padding: 32px;
    border: 4px solid #0055A4;
    border-radius: 12px;
    background: #FFFFFF;
    box-shadow: 0 0 25px #EF4135;
}
```

What these properties do:

- `position: relative` makes `.game` the reference area.
- `position: absolute` lets the start screen sit over the game.
- `top: 50%` and `left: 50%` move it to the centre.
- `transform` centres the screen itself.
- `z-index: 10` places it above the canvas and other content.

If you want the overlay to cover the entire game area rather than only the panel, use:

```css
#startScreen {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    z-index: 10;

    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;

    background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.92);
}
```

The first version creates a centred panel. The second version creates a full overlay over `.game`.

When the Start button is clicked, hide it with:

```js
startScreen.style.display = "none";
```

Make sure `startScreen` is declared:

```js
const startScreen = document.getElementById("startScreen");
```

Also ensure the `<section id="startScreen">` is inside `<main class="game">`; otherwise, the positioning may not align with the game.

