I found the main problems in [index.html](</Users/nolannlescop/Documents/International/Courses/Web programming I/AI assignment/index.html>).

## 1. You have duplicate IDs

You currently have two elements with:

```html
id="info"
```

and two elements with:

```html
id="restartButton"
```

An `id` must be unique. JavaScript’s `getElementById()` only returns the first matching element, so your game-over restart button is not the one connected to the listener.

Change the game-over section to:

```html
<section id="gameOverScreen">
    <h2>Game Over!</h2>
    <p id="gameOverInfo"></p>
    <button id="gameOverRestartButton">Restart</button>
</section>
```

Keep the original `info`, `restartButton`, and `startButton` IDs for the main game.

## 2. Hide the game-over screen initially

In [style.css](</Users/nolannlescop/Documents/International/Courses/Web programming I/AI assignment/style.css>), add:

```css
#gameOverScreen {
    display: none;
}
```

Place this in the existing `#gameOverScreen` rule rather than creating a second rule.

## 3. Fix the game-over CSS

You currently have this selector:

```css
#gameOverScreen {
    background: #ff1100;
}
```

That changes the entire overlay background to red. It looks like you intended to style the button. Change it to:

```css
#gameOverRestartButton {
    background: #EF4135;
}

#gameOverRestartButton:hover {
    background: rgb(57, 57, 57);
}
```

## 4. Connect the game-over elements

Add these references near your other DOM references:

```js
const gameOverInfo = document.getElementById("gameOverInfo");
const gameOverRestartButton =
    document.getElementById("gameOverRestartButton");
```

Then update `endGame`:

```js
const endGame = () => {
    gameOver = true;
    clearInterval(gameTimer);

    gameOverInfo.textContent =
        "Score: " + score + " | Highest score: " + highestScore;

    gameOverScreen.style.display = "flex";
};
```

Finally, connect the correct button:

```js
gameOverRestartButton.addEventListener("click", restartGame);
```

Your `restartGame()` already contains:

```js
gameOverScreen.style.display = "none";
```

so it should hide the overlay and restart the game.

The key issue was not the collision logic—it was the duplicate IDs. Test this sequence:

1. Start the game.
2. Hit a wall.
3. Confirm the game-over overlay appears.
4. Click the overlay’s Restart button.
5. Confirm the overlay disappears and the snake starts again.

