I found the cause in `restartGame()`:

```js
gameSpeed = 150;
```

That line resets every difficulty to the normal speed. The first game uses `applyDifficulty()`, but the next game overwrites the selected speed with `150`.

Replace it with:

```js
applyDifficulty();
```

In [index.html](</Users/nolannlescop/Documents/International/Courses/Web programming I/AI assignment/index.html>), this part:

```js
food = generateFood();
score = 0;
gameSpeed = 150;
gameOver = false;
```

should become:

```js
applyDifficulty();
food = generateFood();
score = 0;
gameOver = false;
```

The order matters: apply the difficulty before generating the food, because hard mode changes `tileCount`.

Also, your speed calculation currently gets progressively too fast because it subtracts from the already-reduced `gameSpeed`:

```js
gameSpeed = Math.max(50, gameSpeed - score * 5);
```

For consistent difficulty, store the selected base speed:

```js
let baseGameSpeed = 150;
```

Update `applyDifficulty()`:

```js
const applyDifficulty = () => {
    const settings = difficultySettings[difficultySelect.value];

    baseGameSpeed = settings.speed;
    gameSpeed = baseGameSpeed;
    canvas.width = settings.boardSize;
    canvas.height = settings.boardSize;
    tileCount = settings.boardSize / gridSize;
};
```

Then, after eating an apple, calculate from the base speed:

```js
gameSpeed = Math.max(50, baseGameSpeed - score * 5);
```

Now:

- Easy always starts at `200`.
- Normal always starts at `150`.
- Hard always starts at `100`.
- Restarting preserves the selected difficulty.
- Speed increases consistently as the score grows.

Test hard mode twice in a row and check whether both rounds start at the same speed.

