```html
<p id="gameOverInfo">Score: 0 | Highest score: 0</p>``
```

Remove the extra backticks:

```html
<p id="gameOverInfo">Score: 0 | Highest score: 0</p>
```

## Style the score display

In [style.css](</Users/nolannlescop/Documents/International/Courses/Web programming I/AI assignment/style.css>), replace your current `#info` rule with:

```css
#info {
    display: inline-block;
    min-width: 240px;
    margin: 8px 0 16px;
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border: 2px solid #0055A4;
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: #FFFFFF;
    color: #0055A4;
    font-size: 1.1rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    box-shadow: 0 0 10px rgba(0, 85, 164, 0.6);
}
```

This gives the score a visible panel above the canvas.

You can style the game-over score similarly:

```css
#gameOverInfo {
    padding: 10px 18px;
    border-radius: 20px;
    background: #FFFFFF;
    color: #0055A4;
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

## Style the difficulty selector

Add:

```css
#difficulty {
    display: block;
    margin: 14px auto;
    padding: 10px 40px 10px 14px;
    border: 2px solid #0055A4;
    border-radius: 8px;
    background: #FFFFFF;
    color: #0055A4;
    font-size: 1rem;
    font-weight: bold;
    cursor: pointer;
}

#difficulty:hover,
#difficulty:focus {
    border-color: #EF4135;
    outline: none;
    box-shadow: 0 0 8px rgba(239, 65, 53, 0.7);
}
```

The `focus` style makes it clear when the selector is active.

You can also style its label:

```css
#startScreen label {
    display: block;
    margin-top: 20px;
    color: #FFFFFF;
    font-weight: bold;
}
```

Your difficulty selector will then look like:

- Blue border and text normally
- Red highlight when selected
- White background for readability

